#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087542 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "03260b0d-f1c4-5c40-89dc-3de2d1017148" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "b3b024c9-282e-55c3-9300-0db6b88cd3c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6609" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y = 20 e ^ {3x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 1, -2,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $20e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3 \\alpha e ^{3x},\n\\quad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 9  \\alpha e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\alpha {e}^{3x}+3\\alpha {e}^{3x}-2\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =20{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha (9+3-2){e}^{3x} & =20{e}^{3x} \\\\ 10\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =20{e}^{3x} \\\\ 10\\alpha  & =20 \\\\ \\alpha  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is \n\n$$\ny_p = 2e^{3x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087543 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61611329-9ecd-5917-a11e-3b5effa69673" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent"}] :question/id #uuid "0d5c1b8c-6f6e-53d6-b2de-034a3f193fef" :question/math-academy-id "e-6610" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/4652a17e8836f9ea84df971537f3c0df1f5ccd2b2dfd7d8d9615ef69a775b972.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the roots of the characteristic equation are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/bb796871deb9ebb6ff0ac3c98715a74275b8957374753a1bfed998898de136f8.png) we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/fbd5028588b1b3639ae465a5904a49e354da5f77ae0224b398666449dcfd809b.png)\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/4d6d063a30dc8f4bab52eab0764e8dee217ddd74055d79cae2bcca34e46ca45f.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/df86a6554579076b0783330da9971ee6ac847ce0d7615c81da959cc197372021.png) is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/187230e4c3f0192e65cb84e69fa12ece83467ae67ca3f73f09ae636d8eb7daad.png)\n\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/13751f284d6fa34631cd1eb362665db0f1e29de98a1539e514009e7c5034e855.png) is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/6316c498377eadd30922f7f5ef6cfa1015cb3c059349bccf2aec7ca52ea5356e.png) we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/955d1d9ea20426db629258e32703eda1f724e0787bedc68c653eaa684a997f02.png)\n\nTo find the value of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/07f02913cce959d6fde7770ff94b1fbab15d24d8c7f7ee5c8cba48873d955f86.png)  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/0770a9b23e24c99631cdbc12378388ff089585206f0aea21ebfb3f65217cd4a2.png)\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/67180bb752c7046f8b83aadacd7753bed9d827516c4f1930b341873b5399951e.png)\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/dbf2415e9f21a606aa92b6e26582cc1790470343346e967a0ff621c678560fd6.png)\n\nand the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/a197e920c5e9c7d6b16fffcd7955fe07a212b92fb50636feae2c771f91a68f8a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087544 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01e7705-e8a6-5712-ad9a-2fc77ee3cdec" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "d655af30-3da8-50cb-8c06-c3860bf88b5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6611" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the characteristic equation has a single repeated root $\\lambda = 3,$ the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ and $xe^{3x}$ are both terms in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2e ^ {3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(3{x}^{2}+2x)\\alpha {e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=(9{x}^{2}+12x+2)\\alpha {e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $y_p$, $y'_p$ and $y_p''$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{x}^{2}+12x+2)\\alpha {e}^{3x}-6(3{x}^{2}+2x)\\alpha {e}^{3x}+9\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ (9{x}^{2}+12x+2-18{x}^{2}-12x+9{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =1 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac 1 2x^2 e ^{3x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087553 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "749a2a49-4181-5be7-b5ef-3e5f8d82219f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "bbf37829-4b55-5a39-bf20-d067b34db8fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-6787" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\nAe^{3 x} + Bxe^{3 x} + \\dfrac 1 2 x ^2 e^{3 x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) = 2 , \\ \\ y'(0) = 1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A{e}^{0}+B(0){e}^{0}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}{e}^{0} \\\\ 2 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=3A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{3x}+3Bx{e}^{3x}+x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y'(0)=1$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}+3B(0){e}^{0}+0{e}^{0}+\\frac{3}{2}(0)^{2}{e}^{0} \\\\ 1 & =3A+B \\\\ 1 & =3(2)+B \\\\ -5 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=2$ and $B=-5,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2{e}^{3x}-5x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "03260b0d-f1c4-5c40-89dc-3de2d1017148" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70d59520-c2d1-5a4e-af9b-5ac668029bed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350117} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb8f278-50bb-5025-a754-dae70c45b38a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350118} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e1832a-435f-5f76-873f-f51b72d74e2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350119} {:answer/id #uuid "488331d3-871b-5af2-8b4e-04f2d244fe72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=4{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350120} {:answer/id #uuid "40616a1d-e422-55f4-8121-95402df4f3c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbb8f278-50bb-5025-a754-dae70c45b38a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350118} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f319933-2af8-56cb-a1ab-c35ec7698680" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1057cfea-89cf-56ee-9a3c-493daedf6a35" :question/math-academy-id "q-64289" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-9\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+18y=8{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{6x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{6x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =3$ and $\\lambda =6.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8{e}^{2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{2x}-9(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})+18(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =8{e}^{2x} \\\\ (4-18+18)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =8{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =8{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "03260b0d-f1c4-5c40-89dc-3de2d1017148" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7cf3d9d-e0d7-557b-9557-7c98a47abd92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = \\frac{1}{4}xe^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085964} {:answer/id #uuid "cf269196-9459-5e0c-bce4-6671cd13c6cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-\\frac{1}{2}xe^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085965} {:answer/id #uuid "e0cbbd35-e6c9-59ea-9b57-77ae8595de2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085966} {:answer/id #uuid "4a5846b2-f305-5845-a3d1-5e7b0fbf923d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-\\frac{1}{4}e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085967} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf503bf-84ba-5942-ac6f-0e0f632e1c4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-2e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a5846b2-f305-5845-a3d1-5e7b0fbf923d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-\\frac{1}{4}e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085967} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ad9a8ec-fbfb-5c77-adae-d847ff800df8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ce92e68-8f9c-589f-b8b4-6c7d87f99818" :question/math-academy-id "q-64290" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y={e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-4x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =-4.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is ${e}^{-3x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-3$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=9\\alpha {e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+3(-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x})-4(\\alpha {e}^{-3x}) & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ (9-9-4)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ -4\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61611329-9ecd-5917-a11e-3b5effa69673" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1b52098-f7bc-5008-9972-71c0b48c9e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350124} {:answer/id #uuid "c03c4a47-5261-540d-a442-0ed71ce78af0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350125} {:answer/id #uuid "a7420652-c3f5-5039-b913-f13b6531491c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350126} {:answer/id #uuid "e35fa4a3-9020-5999-814d-5380dc95b75f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-3x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350127} {:answer/id #uuid "3a880f9e-da70-5bf4-9cd8-abb69eccf275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1b52098-f7bc-5008-9972-71c0b48c9e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350124} :answer-field/id #uuid "2957d7f6-2087-533f-8da9-8b91b8845792" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f55c38ad-8baa-564e-9bec-31da8339ebdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-64292" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=2{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-4x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =-4.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2{e}^{-4x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-4$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (16x-8){e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (16x-8){e}^{-4x}+6(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x})+8(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}) & =2{e}^{-4x} \\\\ (16x-8+6-24x+8x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =2{e}^{-4x} \\\\ -2\\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =2{e}^{-4x} \\\\ -2\\alpha  & =2 \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01e7705-e8a6-5712-ad9a-2fc77ee3cdec" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6004929b-450c-507b-bedb-43dd04ccb911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350131} {:answer/id #uuid "30dd42aa-9f7e-5c91-accb-9e3ae0b9d6c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350132} {:answer/id #uuid "d852390a-64c2-5027-97fa-e79ef3533177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350133} {:answer/id #uuid "7da87c26-752d-54f6-9002-89434bc27e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350134} {:answer/id #uuid "53d6dd22-0578-5b24-9c4c-65fb7162c89e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7da87c26-752d-54f6-9002-89434bc27e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350134} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2dbed1a-794c-51fe-86d4-c0ecfe6478b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9111b9e4-c234-557d-b1f8-8c4096c02984" :question/math-academy-id "q-64294" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=3{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x},$ we have that $\\lambda =2$ is the double root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $3{e}^{2x}.$  In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (2{x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (4{x}^{2}+8x+2){e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (4{x}^{2}+8x+2){e}^{2x}-4(\\alpha (2{x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x})+4(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{2x}) & =3{e}^{2x} \\\\ (4{x}^{2}+8x+2-8{x}^{2}-8x+4{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =3 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "749a2a49-4181-5be7-b5ef-3e5f8d82219f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca136206-5dca-5ffc-ba32-1504337baa86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2e^{-3x} + 3e^{4x} - e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086929} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3a7c34-8d45-5a28-b8fe-76d6764a4326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-e^{-3x} + 2e^{4x} - e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086930} {:answer/id #uuid "488fd97a-a91e-5988-9f6c-a35c658b59c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3e^{4x} + 2e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086931} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb1952b-e027-531f-a87d-4d56d69c8de5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-3x} + 3e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086932} {:answer/id #uuid "754b3c6b-2d2a-5849-a654-cc018c9ca2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{4x} - e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "754b3c6b-2d2a-5849-a654-cc018c9ca2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{4x} - e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086933} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ae52ace-bd46-5976-9811-9986cba3b879" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "713c901d-e72c-54a4-8aba-92a200e2d2b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-8100" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-12y=10{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=0,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =4$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10{e}^{2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{2x}-2\\alpha {e}^{2x}-12(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha (4-2-12){e}^{2x} & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ -10\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ -10\\alpha  & =10 \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{4x}-{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}+4B{e}^{4x}-2{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3A+4B-2 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=1 \\\\ -3A+4B=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=0,B=1.$\n\nWe substitute this into the general particular integral and find that the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}-{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01e7705-e8a6-5712-ad9a-2fc77ee3cdec" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef37b2f1-dfb8-5a6d-9bc4-8e857188fbf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}-{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350138} {:answer/id #uuid "43d82bf4-c46a-5160-9e87-608da6ee4d67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}+{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350139} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8cb1a9-1aaf-5fec-95b8-31d79689847e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x/2}+B{e}^{-x/2}+{e}^{3x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350140} {:answer/id #uuid "04b775c9-bf23-50c9-bcb5-2c64b2b1bd28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x/2}+B{e}^{-3x/2}+2x{e}^{3x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350141} {:answer/id #uuid "3495db7a-4b13-57d5-b796-6dadbc8677a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3495db7a-4b13-57d5-b796-6dadbc8677a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350142} :answer-field/id #uuid "e77ab864-53e2-5c19-96d1-080ffaeb79f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a5802fd-37fc-583e-8254-281bf6bcccd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-8102" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{9}{4}y=6{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{9}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{3x/2}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $\\frac{3}{2}$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (2x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (2+6x+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (2+6x+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2}-3(\\alpha (2x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2})+\\frac{9}{4}(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}) & =6{e}^{3x/2} \\\\ (2+6x+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}-6x-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{3x/2} & =6{e}^{3x/2} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{3x/2} & =6{e}^{3x/2} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "03260b0d-f1c4-5c40-89dc-3de2d1017148" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095c6f84-a92f-5535-8684-c41d550e9a49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-5x/2}+B{e}^{5x/2}-\\frac{4}{7}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350145} {:answer/id #uuid "8359288a-a3b2-55cc-98af-f290bc634e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-5x/2}+B{e}^{5x/2}+\\frac{8}{49}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350146} {:answer/id #uuid "3530c584-6328-512b-adeb-a33287509755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}-\\frac{4}{49}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350147} {:answer/id #uuid "468cac85-0e6b-5c0c-b395-0e773de0d123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}+\\frac{2}{7}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350148} {:answer/id #uuid "81c681ec-4ff9-582e-91da-0ad08c942195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}+\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81c681ec-4ff9-582e-91da-0ad08c942195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}+\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350149} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcff9fc4-517f-578a-b6f7-577b3359653b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c248c7e-6807-55f8-be03-cfefc78d425e" :question/math-academy-id "q-8103" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{25y}{4}=4{e}^{-x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{25y}{4}=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\frac{5}{2}$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-1$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha {e}^{-x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha {e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha {e}^{-x}-5(-\\alpha {e}^{-x})+\\frac{25}{4}\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ (1+5+\\frac{25}{4})\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{49}{4}\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{49}{4}\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{16}{49}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}+\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "749a2a49-4181-5be7-b5ef-3e5f8d82219f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b56ac430-084f-532f-a4c5-d2f01f4efbf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-3x+\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+3{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350152} {:answer/id #uuid "3aef229d-b47c-5261-a07b-127d811e5ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350153} {:answer/id #uuid "5af595e9-1461-566f-a4b1-b57e53c5559e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-3x+\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350154} {:answer/id #uuid "0c4088f4-fce9-5681-b090-4fed673a1c49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350155} {:answer/id #uuid "057f689b-687d-561f-8765-3f174bafeb13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-3x+\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5af595e9-1461-566f-a4b1-b57e53c5559e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-3x+\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350154} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d6934a9-c07a-5561-920e-e9a92cfb5478" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5718b8be-20aa-5d90-b245-2b6bfc4ed681" :question/math-academy-id "q-8108" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+10y=-6{e}^{-2x},\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi },\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2{e}^{-\\pi }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+10y=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(x) & ={e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-6{e}^{-2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}+6(-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x})+10(\\alpha {e}^{-2x}) & =-6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (4-12+10)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =-6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =-6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-3{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi }$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-3\\pi /2}-3{e}^{-\\pi } & ={e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ Q{e}^{-3\\pi /2} & =4{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ Q & =4{e}^{\\pi /2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2{e}^{-\\pi }.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(Q-3P){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(P+3Q){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2{e}^{-\\pi }$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(P+3Q){e}^{-3\\pi /2}+6{e}^{-\\pi } & =-2{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -(P+12{e}^{\\pi /2}){e}^{-3\\pi /2} & =-8{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -P{e}^{-3\\pi /2}-12{e}^{-\\pi } & =-8{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -P{e}^{-3\\pi /2} & =4{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ P & =-4{e}^{\\pi /2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-4{e}^{\\pi /2}$ and $Q=4{e}^{\\pi /2}.$\n\nWe substitute this into the general particular integral and find that the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(-4{e}^{\\pi /2}){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(4{e}^{\\pi /2}){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{e}^{-2x} \\\\ y & =4{e}^{-3x+\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "03260b0d-f1c4-5c40-89dc-3de2d1017148" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27dbdc82-7de0-5db3-a009-698a3082052e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P{e}^{2x}+Q{e}^{-2x}-x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350159} {:answer/id #uuid "8c358ec7-bad2-5078-8751-207c568132f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350160} {:answer/id #uuid "03bd4e83-74f7-5112-9a3f-a5c8dbf5f7a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P{e}^{x}+Qx{e}^{x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350161} {:answer/id #uuid "17c67761-3b91-512a-86b1-1367e898ca83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+5x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350162} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7c4e61-7b14-55cc-af2d-4870b6c16baf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-2{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c358ec7-bad2-5078-8751-207c568132f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350160} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cfcb026-536c-5ba9-8c00-5b9c789ee138" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a50bb5dc-9198-5fcf-9e92-5a19a3bdc9f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-93673" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=20{e}^{4x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +5=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $20{e}^{4x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $4$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=16\\alpha {e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha {e}^{4x}-4(4\\alpha {e}^{4x})+5(\\alpha {e}^{4x}) & =20{e}^{4x} \\\\ (16-16+5)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =20{e}^{4x} \\\\ 5\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =20{e}^{4x} \\\\ 5\\alpha  & =20 \\\\ \\alpha  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "03260b0d-f1c4-5c40-89dc-3de2d1017148" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f32a9e0d-f302-55c5-a5bd-fd6f0e760951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350166} {:answer/id #uuid "c73ad300-8c72-50f3-a6bc-13dedafd9a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-3x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350167} {:answer/id #uuid "d13580aa-7ff6-53b6-9113-5f2b3a26d41d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350168} {:answer/id #uuid "fc52a848-2e6c-5455-ac1a-e2c9e0b33def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350169} {:answer/id #uuid "a89a606f-d688-58ab-83b5-f801bf70308a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d13580aa-7ff6-53b6-9113-5f2b3a26d41d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350168} :answer-field/id #uuid "118bb02c-7b00-5095-ad27-28d4515c5668" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "667003ae-52b1-572c-b234-cf239bc6b6d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-93675" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=12{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $12{e}^{-2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}+4(-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x})+8(\\alpha {e}^{-2x}) & =12{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (4-8+8)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =12{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =12{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61611329-9ecd-5917-a11e-3b5effa69673" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88c0c6ad-19b0-5966-8897-6ce8400587be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350173} {:answer/id #uuid "af738071-c5ad-51f1-b3eb-08cf75db9ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350174} {:answer/id #uuid "68e9496f-8192-5133-aeed-6e67dcdd2768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-5x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350175} {:answer/id #uuid "deb13f75-d58b-5e27-8aeb-dbd87db5306a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-5{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350176} {:answer/id #uuid "ca65ff4c-5e5b-5f0e-bf09-9e0aac2e92b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca65ff4c-5e5b-5f0e-bf09-9e0aac2e92b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350177} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b33b88c-0891-56ce-b86d-62e75dafbb2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b0d7eeb-30c2-58fb-b039-5edb4ff9136a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93678" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=10{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{4x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\lambda =4.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10{e}^{-x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-1$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1-x){e}^{-x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (x-2){e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (x-2){e}^{-x}-3(\\alpha (1-x){e}^{-x})-4(\\alpha x{e}^{-x}) & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ (x-2-3+3x-4x)\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ -5\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ -5\\alpha  & =10 \\\\ \\alpha  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x{e}^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61611329-9ecd-5917-a11e-3b5effa69673" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22944d33-8918-5daa-8d7d-8cdeae652384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+6x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350180} {:answer/id #uuid "32a30ef0-463c-56f1-b049-679c9146e394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350181} {:answer/id #uuid "22efc29d-b776-5b46-9a4b-a8541e740a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350182} {:answer/id #uuid "90cef367-d458-55c6-b7d0-db4e2db7f145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350183} {:answer/id #uuid "41e6dde8-59bf-59c6-828c-d218d6abac81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22944d33-8918-5daa-8d7d-8cdeae652384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+6x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350180} :answer-field/id #uuid "86b85751-4127-53cb-9888-a7cfaa5f9bd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "feda8138-bd1a-5735-8f46-9588cc9d6c9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-93680" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=6{e}^{3x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =2$ or $\\lambda =3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{3x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $3$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (9x+6){e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p}$, ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (9x+6){e}^{3x}-5(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})+6(\\alpha x{e}^{3x}) & =6{e}^{3x} \\\\ (9x+6-5-15x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =6{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =6{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+6x{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61611329-9ecd-5917-a11e-3b5effa69673" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe78f13e-877f-5b48-a1bc-e7089c8ccbd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-x}+4x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350187} {:answer/id #uuid "20ce795d-937f-5cc5-96cc-961b233e248c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350188} {:answer/id #uuid "c03ae583-3a64-517a-a2cd-e77866f10014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{x}-4{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350189} {:answer/id #uuid "10c520ce-4e99-5622-87e1-84c05293c970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350190} {:answer/id #uuid "c906e411-411c-5ef4-b71b-5a5fff5f7bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-x}+2x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe78f13e-877f-5b48-a1bc-e7089c8ccbd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-x}+4x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350187} :answer-field/id #uuid "d196286f-5f47-55b2-a2e4-6fbd9252c511" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c365de3-c119-52dd-962e-42c6ad6481e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-93682" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4{e}^{-x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =-1$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-1$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1-x){e}^{-x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (x-2){e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (x-2){e}^{-x}+3(\\alpha (1-x){e}^{-x})+2(\\alpha x{e}^{-x}) & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ (x-2+3-3x+2x)\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4x{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-x}+4x{e}^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61611329-9ecd-5917-a11e-3b5effa69673" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd8c57b8-aaa7-5736-a8a7-7d09251a533a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{x} + Be^{2x} - 6e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087295} {:answer/id #uuid "be7c78a4-45de-57ba-928b-caf835175787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-x} + Be^{3x} - 2xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087296} {:answer/id #uuid "e38c0013-cb5a-5c9c-8493-fdf8110a9150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{x} + Be^{2x} + 4e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087297} {:answer/id #uuid "17bb9ab2-b7b7-5e57-96b9-3f888ff7a7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-x} + Be^{3x} + 8xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087298} {:answer/id #uuid "79734a29-5f68-575d-bc89-bd11ad48e86f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-x} + Be^{3x} + xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79734a29-5f68-575d-bc89-bd11ad48e86f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-x} + Be^{3x} + xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087299} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a3245f9-8e44-51b1-866c-483443911fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12c65d94-3bf2-5a92-9d45-1b88fda82190" :question/math-academy-id "q-93683" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=4{e}^{3x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1$ or $\\lambda =3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{3x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $3$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (9x+6){e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (9x+6){e}^{3x}-2(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})-3(\\alpha x{e}^{3x}) & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (9x+6-2-6x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ 4\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}+x{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01e7705-e8a6-5712-ad9a-2fc77ee3cdec" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d16042df-1fc8-55f6-9a6f-8e9801cac98e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350194} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8afba0-184c-59b2-a7da-f48853bcd2eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350195} {:answer/id #uuid "4fcdaebb-ff1d-51f5-b3f7-72d73eeefb28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350196} {:answer/id #uuid "45af9035-4904-5cfe-80fb-6dcbb08b123a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-3x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350197} {:answer/id #uuid "c590d0dd-1711-5f33-9c20-33b1e0ed6602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f8afba0-184c-59b2-a7da-f48853bcd2eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350195} :answer-field/id #uuid "62adf584-d6fd-5005-9fca-95d7054fdecd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec1ebe33-0bb3-5ea6-a15e-48fad1ecf313" :question/math-academy-id "q-93686" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=6{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x},$ we have that $\\lambda =-3$ is the double root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{-3x}.$  In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-3$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (2x-3{x}^{2}){e}^{-3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (9{x}^{2}-12x+2){e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (9{x}^{2}-12x+2){e}^{-3x}+6(\\alpha (2x-3{x}^{2}){e}^{-3x})+9(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}) & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (9{x}^{2}-12x+2+12x-18{x}^{2}+9{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01e7705-e8a6-5712-ad9a-2fc77ee3cdec" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c50590-2cc3-56d0-91d3-7e6a960981e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350201} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3b7c3e-4ad4-5d57-b949-d149be5d35ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350202} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b07144-0294-56f8-9285-1cb3ec7d8c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-4{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350203} {:answer/id #uuid "08648012-10ed-5661-be6e-35987d836c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350204} {:answer/id #uuid "c0290b0c-7ec0-5540-8e4b-75d9ee2e797f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=4{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08648012-10ed-5661-be6e-35987d836c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350204} :answer-field/id #uuid "6daf7e12-a358-5654-ad7e-8544adbf1a49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bdcc65d-ace5-50fe-a859-cbc9e6149544" :question/math-academy-id "q-93687" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=4{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}+Bx{e}^{-5x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}+Bx{e}^{-5x},$ we have that $\\lambda =-5$ is the double root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-5x}.$  In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-5$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (2x-5{x}^{2}){e}^{-5x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (25{x}^{2}-20x+2){e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (25{x}^{2}-20x+2){e}^{-5x}+10(\\alpha (2x-5{x}^{2}){e}^{-5x})+25(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}) & =4{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (25{x}^{2}-20x+2+20x-50{x}^{2}+25{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =4{e}^{-5x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =4{e}^{-5x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01e7705-e8a6-5712-ad9a-2fc77ee3cdec" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce4f1de3-58e6-5672-9ec1-6461caa7fe83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{2x}+3x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350208} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a28033-3c1e-5ee6-bf76-ffd508fbdc56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}-4{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350209} {:answer/id #uuid "4b653edc-78e8-5ab8-a710-d8176c5513e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{2x}-x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350210} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa2d155-fe84-570f-9367-4d597acd1fad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350211} {:answer/id #uuid "0c9aec8f-8670-572b-a478-b9995c029df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{2x}+4x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fa2d155-fe84-570f-9367-4d597acd1fad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350211} :answer-field/id #uuid "989ddf36-9815-5d7a-8094-6347081cf20e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b1ac2a7-38c5-50ac-b0e1-9c8eb0787f12" :question/math-academy-id "q-93689" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=4{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =\\alpha (2x-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (4{x}^{2}-8x+2){e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (4{x}^{2}-8x+2){e}^{-2x}+4\\alpha (2x-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x}+4\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha (4{x}^{2}-8x+2+8x-8{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}){e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "749a2a49-4181-5be7-b5ef-3e5f8d82219f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bbe1e30-668f-5d83-a487-2fef4173e3ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350215} {:answer/id #uuid "d3db79fe-8a63-55bb-a169-856038b215d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350216} {:answer/id #uuid "d186b83b-140f-5608-8c09-4c15a8a2a8fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}+4x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350217} {:answer/id #uuid "400b1c39-5a28-50bf-91b4-91220398b893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350218} {:answer/id #uuid "c085448b-3cea-5c1c-a2b9-3c22978c8f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-3x}-2{e}^{2x}+4{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d186b83b-140f-5608-8c09-4c15a8a2a8fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}+4x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350217} :answer-field/id #uuid "b533751f-14ae-5d39-be16-eb6ba3e10f8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b976d07-2159-5a25-8c79-4392e1bd3d72" :question/math-academy-id "q-93691" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=4{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=4{e}^{-2x},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-3(0)}+B{e}^{-2(0)}+4(0){e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}-2B{e}^{-2x}-8x{e}^{-2x}+4{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=1$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-3A{e}^{-3(0)}-2B{e}^{-2(0)}-8(0){e}^{-2(0)}+4{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ 1 & =-3A-2B+4 \\\\ -3 & =-3A-2B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=1 \\\\ -3A-2B=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1,B=0.$\n\nFinally, since $A=1$ and $B=0,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+4x{e}^{-2x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "749a2a49-4181-5be7-b5ef-3e5f8d82219f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39380810-f9f9-5386-a77c-1b5efc87dafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-3x}+2x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350222} {:answer/id #uuid "04e3d8df-0284-53c5-b2c5-2e7fb66ead6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350223} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a59840-5572-57e4-9107-3d8dc820e936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350224} {:answer/id #uuid "ea926e9b-8273-529b-b8e6-17ae0b92d0d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350225} {:answer/id #uuid "aa96c2f8-08c4-5858-bed7-d22a2a878ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}+3{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa96c2f8-08c4-5858-bed7-d22a2a878ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}+3{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350226} :answer-field/id #uuid "8333d15e-ba6f-56a5-a30e-d66d448a2056" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2c64f62-0e70-5fc9-810e-3f87da8d1c55" :question/math-academy-id "q-93692" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=8{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=8{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =A{e}^{-3(0)}+B{e}^{0}+2(0){e}^{0} \\\\ 4 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}+2{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =-3A{e}^{-3(0)}+B{e}^{0}+2(0){e}^{0}+2{e}^{0} \\\\ 2 & =-3A+B+2 \\\\ 0 & =-3A+B \\\\ B & =3A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=4 \\\\ B=3A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1,B=3.$\n\nFinally, since $A=1$ and $B=3,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+3{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "749a2a49-4181-5be7-b5ef-3e5f8d82219f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7a4ca0f-1477-5bcd-9f14-b65f20f6b290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}-3x{e}^{-x}-2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350229} {:answer/id #uuid "086760f0-4c66-5ce3-a6b1-c985aa20f259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350230} {:answer/id #uuid "41f9204e-9763-5bdc-a87e-7d7366f4d64e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}-2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350231} {:answer/id #uuid "360c12e3-99d3-5277-a391-e597d2dc5185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350232} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d6bb53-9f20-5256-a4a0-29bc2c5f24f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "360c12e3-99d3-5277-a391-e597d2dc5185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350232} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbb42246-9fb5-5412-bced-3cb75be5fceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ea3e699-8539-58fa-9dc2-07fc9097ea9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-93840" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-2{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-2{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =A{e}^{0}+B(0){e}^{0}-2{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 0 & =A-2 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+B{e}^{x}-4{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =A{e}^{0}+B(0){e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}-4{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 0 & =A+B-4 \\\\ 0 & =2+B-4 \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=2$ and $B=2,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}\n$$"}]}
